# Git hooks

Configured path: `.githooks` via `core.hooksPath`.

## Supported push hook

`pre-push-manifest-refresh` is the supported client-side push hook. It
refreshes existing manifest rows and auto-stages the manifest before push.
Absent rows are not added automatically; that remains an owner policy
decision.

## Why there is no `post-push`

Git's authoritative `githooks(5)` documentation defines `pre-push` as the
client-side hook invoked by `git push` (documented in the push-hook section,
lines 197-212 of the fetched version). It does not define a client-side
`post-push` hook. `post-receive` and `post-update` are server-side hooks run by
`git-receive-pack`; GitHub-hosted remotes do not provide this repository with a
custom server hook for our smoke script.

Therefore the VPS system crontab is the sole post-push smoke trigger:

```text
*/5 * * * * /home/ubuntu/.hermes/scripts/post_push_smoke.sh >> /home/ubuntu/.hermes/logs/post-push-smoke.cron.log 2>&1
```

The smoke receipt separates:

- `remote_main_sha`: source publication on GitHub;
- `deployed_runtime_sha`: the last approved live deployment reference;
- `NOOP`: service healthy, no runtime drift, and source/live references equal;
- `WARN`: service healthy but documented runtime drift exists;
- `FAIL`: service/routing/bridge failure, unreadable remote/deployed reference,
  or `/tmp` threshold breach.
